"Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41265view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":110}); Total lines: 1041\nL42:       3. cite32†PI improves sample efficiency, but its benefit fades once the policy has learned from it. L43:   6. cite33†5 Which Offline Metric Should Guide PI Choice? L44:     1. cite34†5.1 ALFWorld-Passcode: Leaked Information Can Distort PI Utility L45:       1. cite35†ALFWorld-Passcode. L46:       2. cite36†A PI can score near-perfect utility while teaching nothing. L47:       3. cite37†The key changes utility; the trajectory changes learning. L48:     2. cite38†5.2 Selecting PI per Task with Offline Metrics L49:       1. cite39†Setup. L50:       2. cite40†Error-step KL contrast is more effective when PI candidates vary in source or strength. L51:       3. cite41†Reverse KL helps only when PI candidates are strong and come from one source. L52:     3. cite42†5.3 Practical Recommendations L53:   7. cite43†6 Related Work L54:     1. cite44†On-policy self-distillation and PI construction. L55:     2. cite45†Combining OPSD with reinforcement learning. L56:   8. cite46†7 Conclusion L57:   9. cite47†References L58:   10. cite48†A PI Construction L59:     1. cite49†Reference trajectories. L60:     2. cite50†Trajectory formats. L61:     3. cite51†Hints. L62:   11. cite52†B Experimental Details L63:     1. cite53†B.1 Benchmarks L64:       1. cite54†ALFWorld. L65:       2. cite55†AppWorld. L66:       3. cite56†$\\tau^{2}$-bench (retail). L67:     2. cite57†B.2 Training Configuration L68:     3. cite58†B.3 Evaluation L69:     4. cite59†B.4 GRPO and GRPO $+$ OPSD L70:   12. cite60†C Offline Metric Protocols L71:     1. cite61†C.1 PI Utility L72:     2. cite62†C.2 Sampled-token Reverse KL L73:     3. cite63†C.3 Error-step KL Contrast L74:   13. cite64†D How Information Leakage Distorts PI Utility L75:     1. cite65†ALFWorld: the model copies the goal-completing action. L76:     2. cite66†$\\tau^{2}$-retail: the 4B thinking student reports a result instead of producing it. L77:     3. cite67†Implication. L78:   14. cite68†E PI Ablations L79:     1. cite69†All three metrics flag mismatched and other-domain PI. L80:     2. cite70†A wrong PI can still produce a large shift. L81:     3. cite71†PI length is not the main driver of the KL metrics. L82:   15. cite72†F Per-Task PI Selection L83:     1. cite73†Pools. L84:     2. cite74†Scoring. L85:     3. cite75†Training. L86:   16. cite76†G Additional Results L87:     1. cite77†G.1 OPSD Performance L88:     2. cite78†G.2 Offline Metrics L89:   17. cite79†H Prompts and Example Hints L90:     1. cite80†H.1 Agent Prompts L91:       1. cite81†ALFWorld. L92:       2. cite82†AppWorld. L93:       3. cite83†$\\tau^{2}$-retail. L94:       4. cite84†Teacher context. L95:     2. cite85†H.2 Hint-Generation Prompts L96:       1. cite86†H.2.1 Summary L97:       2. cite87†H.2.2 Skill L98:       3. cite88†H.2.3 Checklist L99:     3. cite89†H.3 Example Hints L100:       1. cite90†ALFWorld. L101:       2. cite91†AppWorld. L102:       3. cite92†$\\tau^{2}$-retail. L103: cite93†License: CC BY-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L104: \nL105: arXiv:2610.22512v1 [cs.LG] 15 Oct 2026\nL106: \nL107: \\papertype\nL108: \nL109: Scale AI Research \\contact Code and data: cite94†https://github.com/zkjzou/PI-OPSD†github.com L110: \nL111: ^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{∗}Corresponding authors: zkjzou@umich.edu, anas.mahmoud@scale.com.^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{†}Work done during an internship at Scale AI.\nL112: # Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation\nL113: \nL114: Kaijian Zou Affiliation: University of Michigan – Ann Arbor    Emiliano Penaloza Affiliation: Mila    MohammadHossein Rezaei Affiliation: Scale AI    Anisha Gunjal Affiliation: Scale AI    Mohit Raghavendra Affiliation: Scale AI    Lu Wang Affiliation: University of Michigan – Ann Arbor    Yunzhong He Affiliation: Scale AI    Anas Mahmoud Affiliation: Scale AI\nL115: ###### Abstract\nL116: On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) enables large language models (LLMs) to learn from privileged information (PI) without a separate teacher model. However, prior work largely treats PI as a fixed design choice, leaving the effects of its content and construction poorly understood. This challenge is particularly important in multi-turn agentic settings, where trajectories contain actions, observations, and reasoning that can be transformed into PI in many ways.\nL117: We systematically evaluate 16 PI configurations across three multi-turn agentic benchmarks and three student models that vary in scale and reasoning mode. We find that PI effectiveness depends on its content, generation source, and the student model. Information-dense trajectories and PI produced by more capable generators generally yield larger gains, whereas thinking models benefit less consistently.\nL118: To assess PI before training, we compare PI utility with two KL-based metrics: sampled-token reverse KL ($D$) and error-step KL contrast ($\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$). The KL-based metrics track post-training performance more consistently than PI utility, which can be distorted by information leakage. Empirically, selecting each task’s PI by $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ also improves post-training performance over random selection, most when the PI candidates differ widely.\nL119: Finally, effective PI remains beneficial when combined with GRPO, allowing the combined objective to outperform a strong GRPO baseline. Overall, our results provide practical guidance for constructing and evaluating PI in OPSD training.\nL120: ## 1 Introduction\nL121: On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) enables LLMs to iteratively improve by turning successful solutions, environment feedback, and experience from their own interactions into training signals without a separate teacher model [cite95†19 , cite96†35 , cite97†7 , cite98†22 ]. Related self-distillation methods have already been used to train coding agents such as Composer 2.5 and agents for long-horizon professional tasks [cite99†4 , cite100†8 ].\nL122: In OPSD, the model learns from its own trajectories by matching the same model conditioned on PI, thereby internalizing information unavailable at inference time.\nL123: The effectiveness of OPSD, however, depends on what the teacher is allowed to see. In general, PI can take many forms, including ground-truth answers [cite96†35 ], demonstrations from prior tasks [cite98†22 ], environment feedback [cite97†7 ], actions from successful trajectories [cite95†19 ], or hints derived from gold solutions or trajectories, such as summaries [cite95†19 ], skills [cite101†26 ], checklists, structured criteria such as rubrics [cite102†20 , cite103†6 ], and targeted feedback [cite104†33 , cite99†4 ].\nL124: These choices determine both the information available to the teacher and how that information changes its distribution relative to the student. However, especially on multi-turn agentic tasks, existing studies evaluate only a limited range of PI formats across a small set of models and tasks [cite105†16 , cite106†29 , cite101†26 , cite104†33 , cite107†30 ].\nL125: It remains unclear what makes PI produce useful learning signals for the student policy, whether the same choices work across agentic domains and model types, and, critically, whether PI quality can be assessed before training. Reliable offline metrics would allow candidate PI formats and sources to be compared before training, avoiding a separate costly OPSD run for every candidate and focusing compute on the most promising PI.\nL126: Figure 1: Overview of the PI formats and offline metrics studied. Left—PI formats: reference trajectories retain actions (A), add observations (A+O) or thoughts (A+T), or include both (A+O+T). Compressed hints include a plain Summary, a Skill with mistake analysis and reusable strategies, and a Checklist of task requirements derived from reference and failed student trajectories. Right—offline metrics: PI utility measures the teacher’s reward gain from PI, but leakage can inflate or deflate it.\nL127: Sampled-token reverse KL $D$ measures shift magnitude; better PI is generally associated with a larger $D$, but $D$ does not indicate whether the shift is beneficial. Error-step KL contrast $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ measures whether that shift targets the identified errors.\nL128: We study these questions through a systematic evaluation of OPSD on three multi-turn agentic benchmarks: ALFWorld [cite108†24 ], AppWorld [cite109†25 ], and $\\tau^{2}$-retail [cite110†2 ]. Figure cite111†1 summarizes the PI formats and offline metrics evaluated in our study. We cover 16 PI configurations spanning reference trajectories and self- and expert-generated hints, including summaries, skills, and checklists.\nL129: We train Qwen3-4B-Instruct, Qwen3-4B-Thinking, and Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct [cite112†28 ], allowing us to compare PI effects across instruction and thinking models and across model scales.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41265view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 1284\nL40:     5. cite30†5.5 Ablations L41:   7. cite31†6 Discussion L42:     1. cite32†Limitations. L43:   8. cite33†References L44:   9. cite34†A Formal 