ss-floor calibration L84:     6. cite74†D.6 Methods outside the comparison L85:       1. cite75†SimSD. L86:       2. cite76†MRP, from reported numbers. L87:     7. cite77†D.7 Full-attention targets (LLaDA-8B, Dream-7B) L88:     8. cite78†D.8 Accounting details L89:   13. cite79†E Datasets L90:   14. cite80†F Extended results L91:     1. cite81†F.1 Extended comparison against related methods L92:     2. cite82†F.2 Oracle grid L93:     3. cite83†F.3 More models L94:       1. cite84†The smallest target is where padding costs most. L95:       2. cite85†The full transfer matrix. L96:     4. cite86†F.4 More block sizes L97:     5. cite87†F.5 Generation length L98:     6. cite88†F.6 More ablations and curves L99:       1. cite89†Full budget grid. L100:       2. cite90†Budget curves. L101:       3. cite91†Temperature sweep. L102:     7. cite92†F.7 Additional verifier material L103:     8. cite93†F.8 Mechanism: what happens in deep blocks L104:     9. cite94†F.9 Error analysis of the drafter L105:     10. cite95†F.10 Drafter architecture L106:   15. cite96†G Reproducibility L107:     1. cite97†Seeds and wall clock. L108:     2. cite98†Oracle pairing. L109:     3. cite99†Hardware. L110:   16. cite100†H Terms, common questions and released resources L111:     1. cite101†Keywords. L112:     2. cite102†H.1 Terms L113:     3. cite103†H.2 Common questions L114:       1. cite104†What problem does SPOT solve? L115:       2. cite105†Why is commit order, not the token, the bottleneck? L116:       3. cite106†How does SPOT draft and verify? L117:       4. cite107†Is the output exact when the target samples? L118:       5. cite108†How does SPOT differ from Spiffy, SSD and BlockSpec? L119:       6. cite109†Which models and benchmarks does the paper use? L120:       7. cite110†What are the main results? L121:       8. cite111†Does SPOT work beyond SDAR-8B? L122:       9. cite112†What are the limitations? L123:       10. cite113†Where are the code and the drafters? L124:     4. cite114†H.3 Where this work sits L125: cite115†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L126: \nL127: arXiv:2610.22543v1 [cs.LG] 15 Oct 2026\nL128: # On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decoding\nL129: for Diffusion Language Models\nL130: via Commit-Order Prediction\nL131: \nL132: Eliezer Shlomi Affiliation: Technion – Israel Institute of Technology    Eliron Rahimi Affiliation: Technion – Israel Institute of Technology    Avi Mendelson Affiliation: Technion – Israel Institute of Technology    Chaim Baskin Affiliation: INSIGHT Lab, School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev\nL133: ###### Abstract\nL134: Diffusion language models (DLMs) promise parallel text generation, but in practice face an unresolved speed–quality trade-off. Exact decoding fills in only one masked position per model pass, while faster multi-token decoding schemes are lossy. Speculative decoding is the standard lossless acceleration for autoregressive models. However, it does not transfer directly to DLMs, which must choose both what to generate and where.\nL135: We show that this order is the missing piece: the model’s own predictions from the previous pass already give the right token at $92$–$100\\%$ of positions, but not which position comes next. Based on this observation, we introduce S peculating P ositions O rder T rajectory (SPOT), which learns to predict this order.\nL136: A $0.62$M-parameter model reads only the target’s output distributions and proposes a tree of candidate continuations, and the target verifies all of them in a single pass by checking whether each equals its own next state. On the verification side, our analysis shows that prior block-diffusion verification approaches either assume $T{=}0$ or a sequential proposal interface.\nL137: To support tree-based speculation at any temperature, we propose using coupled-noise match verification for diffusion blocks, following shared-Gumbel-noise coupling approaches. Our comprehensive experiments show that SPOT cuts target passes by up to $6.8\\times$ and reaches up to $3.2\\times$ the throughput of standard decoding, beating the calibrated and training-free drafting baselines in every task and budget at $T{=}0$ and $T{=}1$.\nL138: In addition, we show that the learned drafter transfers across models, demonstrating successful transfer across six different DLMs.\nL139: cite116† Project Page†eliezer318.github.io L140: \nL141: ^{1}^{1}footnotetext: Equal contribution. Correspondence: {eliezer, elironrahimi}@campus.technion.ac.il.\nL142: ## 1 Introduction\nL143: Diffusion language models (DLMs) (cite117†Nie et al., 2025 ; cite118†Arriola et al., 2025 ) generate text by iteratively refining masked token sequences rather than predicting tokens left-to-right. Block-diffusion models make this particularly practical by combining bidirectional denoising within each block with causal generation across blocks (cite118†Arriola et al., 2025 ; cite119†Cheng et al., 2026 ).\nL144: The diffusion paradigm enables new capabilities such as bidirectional attention, iterative refinement, enhanced safety robustness, and the potential to substantially accelerate language generation via parallel decoding  (cite120†Ye et al., 2025 ; cite121†Rahimi et al., 2026 ; cite122†Wu et al., 2026 ). Yet this parallelism comes with a fundamental speed–quality trade-off.\nL145: High-quality diffusion decoding typically uses conservative unmasking and therefore requires many denoising steps, while faster decoding schemes reveal multiple positions per step using confidence-based, top-$k$, or dynamic policies, potentially changing the decoding trajectory and degrading generation quality (cite123†Xu et al., 2026 ; cite124†Pan et al., 2025 ; cite125†Sun et al., 2026 ).\nL146: Figure 1: SPOT overview (red: committed; amber dashed: drafted; grey: masked, with its stale argmax token). (a) The target commits its most confident position per forward: the tokens are largely known in advance, the order is not. (b) SPOT reads a hypothetical prefix’s stale distributions, predicts the next position with a $0.62$M order model, grows a tree of states by prefix mass (edge width), and verifies all of them in one batched forward, committing three positions.\nL147: For autoregressive language models (AR-LMs), speculative decoding provides an elegant lossless solution. A cheap drafter proposes several future tokens, and the target verifies them in parallel while preserving exactly the distribution of draft-free decoding (cite126†Leviathan et al., 2023 ; cite127†Chen et al., 2023 ).\nL148: Recent work has begun bringing this idea to DLMs through self-drafting, draft graphs, learned residual predictors, and auxiliary draft models  (cite128†Gao et al., 2025 ; cite129†Agrawal et al., 2026 ; cite124†Pan et al., 2025 ; cite130†Cui et al., 2026 ; cite123†Xu et al., 2026 ). However, speculation changes fundamentally once attention inside a block is bidirectional. In an autoregressive model, the next position is fixed, so the drafter only needs to predict what token comes next.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41265view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":300}); Total lines: 624\nL287: Because the effective rank of $\\Delta W$ sits close to its noise value, the cutoff it gives is high: on Qwen3-0.6B it keeps 83% of the directions and 98.9% of the update’s energy, so the edit touches only the last 1% (Wu et al. report 98.82% for Qwen3-8B). An edit that small is also fragile in practice: running the edited model in bf16 distorts it by 16% of its size at $\\lambda=0.2$ and 25% at $\\lambda=0.5$ (Appendix cite47†H ).\nL288: We compare edits of the same update on two benchmarks: GSM8K (cite99†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), which is not among Wu et al.’s, and GPQA-Diamond (cite100†Rein et al., 2024 ), which is. On GSM8K we score with pass@$k$ (cite101†Chen et al., 2021 ) and compare: Timber’s tail edit, the same edit with a stable-rank cutoff that scales 53 times more of the update’s energy, and simply scaling the whole update by one factor per matrix (0.75 on average) so that the edited update has the same size as the stable-rank edit.\nL289: We run Timber’s edit both at its recommended $\\lambda=0.2$ and at $\\lambda=0.5$, and use Qwen3’s non-thinking mode, the mode of Wu et al.’s main table.\nL290: On all 1,319 problems, Timber’s edit has no detectable effect at either setting (Table cite98†5 , Figure cite102†5 ). At $\\lambda=0.2$ pass@1 is unchanged ($-0.0$ points; $-0.8$, $+0.8$) and pass@8 moves by $+0.2$ ($-1.4$, $+1.8$); at $\\lambda=0.5$ the changes are $+0.7$ ($-0.0$, $+1.6$) and $+0.8$ ($-0.6$, $+2.3$). The stable-rank edit gives a similar pass@1 and a pass@8 gain of $+1.8$ ($+0.2$, $+3.5$).\nL291: Simply scaling the whole update does better than either tail edit: pass@1 rises by $+3.4$ points ($+2.4$, $+4.5$) and pass@8 by $+2.7$ ($+1.1$, $+4.2$), with nearly the same share of answered samples (96.4% against 97.4%). It makes the update as small as the stable-rank edit does, but evenly from every direction instead of from the tail.\nL292: So on this benchmark the gain comes from making the update smaller, and choosing which directions to shrink by a spectral cutoff, whether eRank or stable rank, adds nothing. Task arithmetic already treats the scale of an update as a knob worth tuning (cite103†Ilharco et al., 2023 ); here that knob alone beats the spectral edits.\nL293: Table 6: The same edits on GPQA-Diamond (cite100†Rein et al., 2024 ), Wu et al.’s benchmark, 198 questions, non-thinking mode with their sampling settings. “Accuracy” is the mean over samples (their Mean@$k$), in percent, as is pass@4; eight samples per question for the unedited model, four for each edit. Differences in points with 95% paired bootstrap intervals over questions.\nL294: edit  | accuracy  | $\\Delta$ accuracy  | pass@4  | $\\Delta$ pass@4\nL295: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL296: none  | 25.9  | –  | 46.6  | –\nL297: tail $\\times 0.2$, eRank cutoff (Timber)  | 26.9  | $+0.9$ ($-2.1$, $+4.0$)  | 46.0  | $-0.6$ ($-5.9$, $+4.8$)\nL298: tail $\\times 0.5$, eRank cutoff (Timber)  | 26.4  | $+0.4$ ($-2.5$, $+3.5$)  | 47.5  | $+0.9$ ($-4.2$, $+6.4$)\nL299: tail $\\times 0.8$, eRank cutoff (Timber)  | 26.9  | $+0.9$ ($-2.0$, $+3.9$)  | 47.0  | $+0.4$ ($-4.8$, $+5.6$)\nL300: tail $\\times 0.2$, stable-rank cutoff  | 21.3  | $-4.6$ ($-9.3$, $+0.2$)  | 48.0  | $+1.4$ ($-6.4$, $+9.4$)\nL301: whole update, scaled to the stable-rank edit’s size  | 22.9  | $-3.1$ ($-7.1$, $+0.9$)  | 54.0  | $+7.5$ ($+0.5$, $+14.3$)\nL302: GPQA-Diamond is where Wu et al. report their largest gain for this model: a mean accuracy of 28.79% against 23.74% unedited, $+5.1$ points. Our unedited model scores 25.9%, about two points above theirs. Timber’s edit, at each of the three $\\lambda$ values they searched, moves accuracy by under a point, and every interval includes zero (Table cite104†6 ).\nL303: We do not observe their gain, but our setup is our own, not their evaluation harness: our prompt and answer format, our answer extraction, four samples per question against their three full repeats, and 2,048 new tokens against their 8,192. Truncation, at least, does not explain the difference.\nL304: The share of samples that end without a final answer is 3.5% for the unedited model and 3.4–4.2% for Timber’s three edits (8.7–8.8% for the two larger edits), so Timber’s edits are cut off no more often than no edit. A rerun of the unedited model and the recommended $\\lambda=0.2$ with the limit doubled to 4,096 tokens, the most this model’s samples fit on our GPU, gives 25.6% against 25.4%, a difference of $-0.3$ points ($-3.5$, $+3.0$), with 2.7–2.9% of samples unfinished (Appendix cite47†H ).\nL305: With 198 questions our intervals are about $\\pm 3$ points, and their $+5.1$, from the same 198 questions, carries a similar uncertainty. Under our settings the intervals exclude a gain of five points; a gain of one to three points is not ruled out.\nL306: Here, unlike on GSM8K, the larger edits hurt: shrinking most of the update, whether by the stable-rank cutoff or evenly, costs 3–5 points of mean accuracy (intervals just reaching zero) and makes the model answer at length, while the uniform version’s pass@4 rises. That pattern, worse single answers but more varied ones, is a change in behaviour, not a clear improvement, and with about thirty comparisons across our edit tables one interval just clearing zero is what chance would give.\nL307: Taken together, the two benchmarks say the same thing about the cutoff. An edit that scales only the last 1% of the update’s energy does nothing we can measure, on GSM8K or on GPQA. Edits that scale a quarter of the update change the model’s behaviour much more, for better (GSM8K) or for worse (GPQA), and the spectral cutoff is not what decides it.\nL308: On the first 300 problems we also ran the extreme $\\lambda=0$, which removes the tail completely (Table cite97†15 ). Removing Timber’s tail, 1.1% of the update’s energy, changes nothing measurable ($+0.5$ points of pass@1). Removing the stable-rank “tail”, which is 59% of the update’s energy and so most of the update, costs 4.2 points against the unedited model and 5.3 against halving it. Removing most of an update hurts; this says little about the weakest directions alone.\nL309: A uniform scaling matched in size to Timber’s edit is almost erased by bf16 storage (88% of the change is lost) and, like Timber’s edit, has no clear effect ($+0.8$; $-0.8$, $+2.4$).\nL310: We did not test thinking mode, where Wu et al. also report gains, and our GPQA intervals are wide. Within those limits, the effective-rank cutoff is not what makes an edit of this kind work.\nL311: ## 6 Related work\nL312: ##### Noise baselines for spectra.\nL313: Comparing a spectrum against what noise would give is old. Parallel analysis (cite56†Horn, 1965 ) keeps only the factors whose eigenvalues beat those of random data, and cite57†Gavish and Donoho (2014) derive the optimal threshold for separating signal singular values from Gaussian noise.\nL314: Random matrix theory has also been used to study trained weight matrices: their spectra depart from those of random matrices in heavy-tailed ways that track training quality (cite58†Martin and Mahoney, 2021 ), while much of the bulk still follows random-matrix statistics (cite59†Thamm et al., 2022 ). cite105†Staats et al. (2023) use those statistics to separate the noise part of a weight matrix from its information, and cite106†Hartford et al. (2024) use them to choose which layers to train.\nL315: For fine-tuning, cite52†Biderman et al. (2024) compared the spectrum of a fully fine-tuned weight matrix with that of the pretrained matrix plus Gaussian noise and found them alike. We compare the update itself with noise, measure by measure, and find that the answer depends on which measure one uses.\nL316: ##### Superficial alignment.\nL317: cite49†Zhou et al. (2023) proposed that alignment mostly selects a format the pretrained model already supports, and cite50†Lin et al. (2024) found that base and aligned models differ mainly on stylistic tokens. Others have questioned how far this goes: safety behaviour can be confined to the first few output tokens (cite107†Qi et al., 2025 ), and post-training keeps improving reasoning with more data (cite108†Raghavendra et al., 2024 ). cite51†Wu et al.\nL318: (2025) looked for weight-level evidence through the effective rank of released models (§cite24†5 ). cite109†Chaudhury (2025) locates the effect of preference tuning in a few middle layers by activation patching, a question about activations that our weight analysis does not address.\nL319: ##### The rank of fine-tuning updates.\n"
